Only one test references it directly. **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** (entire file, 16 lines) — note its comment already describes a target change to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`:
```java
/** customer-code-city: Change the customerCode format to '<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>' where CITY3 is the upper-cased... */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreCustomerCodeHasCityPrefix() throws Exception {
		int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
		JsonNode n = fetchOwner(id);
		assertTrue(n.get("customerCode").asText().matches("[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}")); // <CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>
	}
}
```
The assertion regex `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\d{4}` expects the new three-segment format, which the current generator (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) does not satisfy.

Supporting helpers live in **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`**: `ownerNode()` (lines 117-125) builds a valid owner with `lastName = uniqueLastName()` ("Sur"+letters, lines 79-81) and `city = uniqueCity()` ("Town"+seq, lines 94-96); `createOwnerOk` (160-162), `fetchOwner` (line 200), `extractId` (206), and `fillCity(city, n)` (173-179) which creates n owners in the same city. There are no unit tests for the policy classes themselves — the acceptance suite is the only test coverage.

## 4. The Owner entity

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`** — extends `Person` (which holds `firstName`/`lastName`). Relevant fields:
- `city` (lines 42-44): `@Column(name = "city") @NotEmpty private String city;`
- `customerCode` (lines 57-58): `@Column(name = "customer_code") private String customerCode;`
- Also: `address` (38-40), `telephone` (46-49), `email` (51-52), `registrationDate` (54-55), `householdId` (60-61), `namesakeCount` (63-64).
- Getters/setters: `getCustomerCode`/`setCustomerCode` (74-80), `getCity`/`setCity` (125-131). `lastName` is accessed via inherited `getLastName()` (used in `toString`, line 207).
- `@PrePersist defaultRegistrationDate()` (lines 102-107) defaults `registrationDate` to `LocalDate.now()` — relevant because membershipNumber depends on it.

## 5. Repository methods to count/query owners by city

There are none currently. The `OwnerRepository` interface (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`) exposes only `findByLastName`, `findById`, `save`, `findAll`, `delete` — no city-based method. The Spring Data implementation (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java`, lines 39-57) has no `countByCity`/`findByCity`. `ClinicService` (`.../service/ClinicService.java`, line 57) exposes only `findAllOwners()`.

City appears only as a SELECT column in the JDBC impls (`JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` lines 86, 101, 125, 160, 186, 201; `JdbcPetRepositoryImpl`; `JdbcVisitRepositoryImpl`) and in the update SQL — never in a count/aggregate. So any per-city counting today would go through `clinicService.findAllOwners()` and filter in Java, exactly as `OwnerCustomerCodePolicy` does for the global sequence.

### Key implication for a format change
To implement `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` you would modify `OwnerCustomerCodePolicy.assignCustomerCode` (add a CITY3 segment from `owner.getCity()`), update its javadoc and the `OwnerMembershipPolicy` javadoc example, and note that `membershipNumber` will automatically inherit the new prefix since it prepends the full `customerCode`. No repository change is strictly required unless NNNN should become a per-city sequence (currently it is global via `findAllOwners().size() + 1`).

